Notebook[{
Cell[TextData[StyleBox["INTRODUCTION:\nNumerical v.s. Symbolic\nComputing\n\n\
LT20, 4:00-6:00pm\nWed, 8 Jan 2003",
  FontWeight->"Bold"]], "Title"],

Cell["", "Subtitle"],

Cell["A. Numerical Computation", "Subtitle"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "With some exceptions, most programming languages are designed for \
numerical computing, e.g., Fortran, C, and matlab package. The most obvious \
limitations are (1) limited range of integers, (2) finite accuracy of \
floating-point numbers.",
  StyleBox["\n", "Subtitle"]
}], "Text"],

Cell["B. Symbolic Computing", "Subtitle"],

Cell["\<\
Symbolic computing environment overcomes the two limitations (arbitrary sizes \
for integers, and a user-specified accuracy in floating point calculation).   \
Most importantly, symbolic packages provide the ability of manipulating \
formulas, e.g., differentiation or integration of a function.  The \
disadvantage of symbolic systems is that it is typically many times slower \
than a compiled program.
\
\>", "Text"],

Cell["Symbolic Packages (Languages)", "Subtitle"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "(1)  ",
  StyleBox["LISP",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1]],
  ":  low level but powerful programming language.\n(2)  ",
  StyleBox["Maple, Mathematica",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1]],
  ":  two popular symbolic computing packages.\n(3)  and many other special \
packages: ",
  StyleBox["reduce, axiom",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1]],
  ", etc.\n"
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[StyleBox["How to run Mathematica",
  FontColor->RGBColor[1, 0, 0]]], "Subtitle"],

Cell["A. Text-Based Interfaces", "Subtitle"],

Cell["Log into your UNIX system, type", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(math\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
You are engaged in a dialog with the system, one line at time, RETURN to \
enter an input.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["How to run Mathematica",
    FontColor->RGBColor[1, 0, 0]],
  "\nB. Notebook Interfaces (GUI)"
}], "Subtitle"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Log into your Windows system (Start button, Programs, ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "),  or UNIX GUI, click or type"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(mathematica\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "You can access the help as well as the whole book \"",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ": a system for doing Mathematics by Computer\" by S. Wolfram inside the ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica.",
    FontSlant->"Italic"]
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    RowBox[{\(IMPORTANT : \ You\ execute\ an\ Mathematica\ Expression\ with\),
       " "}], "\n", 
    RowBox[{"\t\t", 
      StyleBox[\(SHIFT - ENTER\),
        FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1]]}]}], "Input"],

Cell["Arbitrary Large Integers", "Subtitle"],

Cell["Simple integer calculations", "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(2 + 2\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(4\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(2^90\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(1237940039285380274899124224\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[BoxData[
    RowBox[{
      StyleBox["+",
        FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1]], 
      RowBox[{",", " ", 
        RowBox[{
          StyleBox["-",
            FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1]], 
          RowBox[{",", " ", 
            RowBox[{
              StyleBox["*",
                FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1]], 
              RowBox[{",", " ", 
                RowBox[{
                  StyleBox["/",
                    FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1]], 
                  RowBox[{",", " ", 
                    RowBox[{
                      RowBox[{
                        StyleBox["^",
                          FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1]], " ", "\n", "for"}], 
                      " ", "add"}], ",", "subtract", ",", "multiply", ",", 
                    " ", "divide", ",", \(and\ power\), 
                    ",", \(as\ \(\(usual\)\(.\)\)\)}]}]}]}]}]}]}]}]], "Input"],

Cell["Space can be used as multiplication sign, e.g.,", "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(2\ 3\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(6\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[StyleBox["[This can cause unintended result as x*y is same as x \
y, but xy without a space between x and y is a single variable called xy.]",
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.333333]]], "Text"],

Cell["\<\
Integer functions (GCD[a,b], greatest common divisor, FactorInteger[a], etc):\
\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(GCD[10, 4]\)], "Input"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(FactorInteger[40]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({{2, 3}, {5, 1}}\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "This means 40 = ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`2\^3\)]],
  " 5."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    StyleBox[\(Function\ arguments\ are\ enclosed\ in\ square\ \(\(brakets\ [\
\  ... \ ]\)\(.\)\)\),
      FontColor->GrayLevel[0.500008]]], "Input"],

Cell["Floating Point with Arbitrary Precision", "Subtitle"],

Cell["Floating point numbers are entered with the decimal point:", "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(2.3\  + \ 1.2\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(3.5`\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(3.14159265358979323850000 - \ Pi\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(3.735661672049679358`3.6773*^-20\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "You can type as many digits as you like, they are all significant.  Pi or \
\[Pi] is a symbolic representation of the exact value of \[Pi] (not an \
approximate of it), i.e., the ratio of circumference to diameter of a circle. \
  Beside Pi, we also have E (such that Log[E] = 1), I = ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`\@\(-1\)\)]],
  ", and Infinity (\[Infinity]), as symbolic math constants.  In summary:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(Pi\ or\ \[Pi]\ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ 3.14159 ... \), "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    \(E\ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ 2.71828 ... \), "\
\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    \(I\ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \@\(-1\)\ \ \((imaginary\ \
unit)\)\), "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    \(Infinity\ \ or\ \ \[Infinity]\ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \)}], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
The function N[...] converts an exact number into floating point. (N[ ] does \
not increase the precision of a number, though).\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(N[Pi, 25]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(3.141592653589793238462643383279502884`25\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[BoxData[{
    RowBox[{
      StyleBox[\(Mathematica' s\ rules\),
        FontSize->14], 
      StyleBox[" ",
        FontSize->14], "\n"}], "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    StyleBox[\(for\ numerical\ \(calculations : \n\t\t\(\((1)\) : \ 
            If\ input\ is\ exact\)\), \ 
      the\ computation\ will\ be\ performed\ exactly . \n\t\t\(\((2)\) : \ 
            If\ input\ is\ approximate\), \ the\ result\ is\ approximate, \ 
      with\ accuracy\ not\ great\ than\ the\ \(\(input\)\(.\)\)\),
      FontSize->18]}], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
Precision[ ] tells you the number of significant figures, the Accuracy[ ] \
tells you the number of digits after the decimal point.  SetPrecision[ ] \
raises precision to required number of significant figures.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Precision[1.34]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(16\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Accuracy[134.0]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(14\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(x\  = \ SetPrecision[1.3, \ 28]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(1.300000000000000044408920985006261616945`28\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Precision[x]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(28\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(y\  = \ x\ *\ 1.0; \ Precision[y]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(16\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Why x has 28 digits of precision but y is only 16?   (This is because ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " works with two types of floating-point numbers, machine precision and \
arbitrary precision.  If input is given with less than machine precision, it \
will take as machine precision.)"
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " has a large collection of math functions such as the table below.  Note \
that all functions take arguments in the form of square brackets, and all \
built-in functions are Capitalized."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    RowBox[{\(Sqrt[x]\), "                 ", 
      StyleBox["square",
        FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1]], 
      StyleBox[" ",
        FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1]], 
      StyleBox["root",
        FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1]], 
      StyleBox[" ",
        FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1]], 
      StyleBox[\((\@x)\),
        FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1]]}], "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    RowBox[{
      RowBox[{\(Exp[x]\), " ", "or", " ", \(E^x\), "     ", 
        StyleBox["exponential",
          FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1]]}], 
      StyleBox[" ",
        FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1]]}], "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    RowBox[{\(Log[x]\), "                    ", 
      StyleBox["natural",
        FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1]], 
      StyleBox[" ",
        FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1]], 
      StyleBox["log",
        FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1]]}], "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    RowBox[{\(Log[b, x]\), "             ", 
      StyleBox["logarithm",
        FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1]], 
      StyleBox[" ",
        FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1]], 
      StyleBox["to",
        FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1]], 
      StyleBox[" ",
        FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1]], 
      StyleBox["base",
        FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1]], 
      StyleBox[" ",
        FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1]], 
      StyleBox["b",
        FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1]]}], "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    RowBox[{\(Sin[x]\), ",", \(Cos[x]\), ",", 
      RowBox[{\(Tan[x]\), "    ", 
        StyleBox["trig",
          FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1]]}]}], "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    RowBox[{\(n!\), "                             ", 
      StyleBox["factorial",
        FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1]]}], "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    RowBox[{
      RowBox[{\(Abs[x]\), "                    ", 
        StyleBox["absolute",
          FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1],
          Background->None], 
        StyleBox[" ",
          FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1],
          Background->None], 
        StyleBox["value",
          FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1],
          Background->None]}], 
      StyleBox[
        RowBox[{
          StyleBox["  ",
            FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1],
            Background->None], 
          StyleBox[" ",
            Background->RGBColor[0, 0, 1]]}]]}], "\[IndentingNewLine]", \(Max[
      a, b,  .. ]\), "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    RowBox[{\(Mod[n, m]\), "             ", 
      StyleBox["n",
        FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1]], 
      StyleBox["  ",
        FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1]], 
      StyleBox["modulo",
        FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1]], 
      StyleBox["  ",
        FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1]], 
      StyleBox["m",
        FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1]]}]}], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
See the mathematica quick reference sheet or Sec.3.2 for more.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell["Symbol Data Type", "Subtitle"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Like any programming languages, ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " provided with the usual data type of integer, floating point numbers and \
advanced structures.   In addition, ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " provides a type called \"Symbol\" for symbolic computation.  For \
example:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(x\  + \ 3  x\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(4\ x\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["\<\
The function Head[...] tells you what data type a particular expression is.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Head[x]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Symbol\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Head[3]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Integer\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Head[3\  + \ I\ ]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Complex\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Head[2.0]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Real\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["Some Examples of Symbolic Computation", "Subtitle"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Expand[\ \((1 + x)\)^2]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(1 + 2\ x + x\^2\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Factor[\ 1\  + \ 4\ x\  + \ 3  x^2]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\((1 + x)\)\ \((1 + 3\ x)\)\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Solve[\ {y^2\  + \ x\  - y\  == 0, \ 
        y\  - \ x^2\ \  + 2 \[Equal] \ 0}, \ {x, y}]\)], "Input"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(D[Sin[x], x]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Cos[x]\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(D[x^2, \ {x, \ 2}]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(2\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Integrate[\ \((1 + x)\)^3/\((1 - x^2)\)^2, \ x]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(-\(2\/\(\(-1\) + x\)\)\) + Log[\(-1\) + x]\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Simplify[Sin[x]^2\  + \ Cos[x]^2]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(1\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["\<\

Graphical Computations
\
\>", "Subtitle"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " supports graphics in a convenient way, as well as more advanced \
programming environment.  A simple plot :"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Plot[\ Sin[x], \ {x, \ \(-Pi\), \ Pi}]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Plot3D[\ 
      Exp[\(-\((x^2 + y^2)\)\)], \ {x, \ \(-2\), 2}, \ {y, \ \(-2\), \ 
        2}]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(g = \ 
      Graphics[\ {\ Circle[{1, 2}, 0.5], \ 
          Line[\ {{0, 0}, \ {1, 0}, \ {1, 1}}]}]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Show[g, \ AspectRatio\  -> \ Automatic]\)], "Input"],

Cell["READINGS", "Title"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Introduction to ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ":  section 1.1 to 1.6\nNotebook interface section 1.3.4 to 1.3.9.\n(Part \
of Chapter 1, A Practical Introduction to ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ").\n\nThe section number refers to the on-line \nas well as printed ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " book sections."
}], "Subtitle"]
},
